          %h2#section_Idea_of_a_situs Idea of a situs
          %p
            A
            %em situs
            is a notion of generalised topological space, and defined as a simplicial object of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+filters"} category of filters on sets
            or, equivalently, the category of finitely additive measures taking values 0 and 1 only, i.e. a simplicial set equipped, for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 0
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sM
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sM
            of
            %em simplicial sets with finitely additive measures
            obtained by dropping the restriction that the measure takes only two values. With appropriate definitions, sዋ is a full subcategory of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sM
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sM
          %p
            Intuitively, these filters (measures) are viewed as additional structure of topological nature on a simplicial set (“the situs structure on a simplicial set”) giving a precise meaning to the phrase “a simplex is sufficiently small”: by definition, we say “a property holds for all small enough
            = succeed "-simplices\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %em neighbourhoods
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{x_0}(x)
            holds for all points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            sufficiently close to a given point
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_0,x)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            near
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            is thought of as small (a small simplex) because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            approximates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            up to a small error.
          %p
            Situses generalise metric and topological spaces, filters, and simplicial sets, and the concept is designed to be flexible enough to formulate categorically a number of standard basic elementary definitions in various fields, e.g. in analysis, limit, (uniform) continuity and convergence, equicontinuity of sequences of functions; in algebraic topology, being locally trivial and geometric realisation; in geometry, quasi-isomorphism; in model theory, stability and simplicity and several Shelah’s dividing lines, e.g. NIP, NOP, NSOP,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi NSOP
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NSOP_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi NTP
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NTP_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NATP
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NATP
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NFCP
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NFCP
          %p
            No homotopy theory for situses has been developed, although the naive definition of an
            %a{:href => "#IntervalSitus"} interval object
            (namely, the simplicial set represented by the linear order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            equipped with some situs structure based on the metric/topology) leads to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+topological+space"} directed (not symmetric) notion of homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#locallytrivial"} (becoming trivial after a certain base-change/pullback)
          %p
          %h2#section_Examples Examples
          %p We now give a number of examples demonstrating the expressive power of the category of situses.
          %p
          %h3#section_Simplicial_sets_as_situses Simplicial sets as situses
          %p
            A simplicial set can be equipped with discrete or indiscrete situs structure: %The (in)discrete situs structure on a simplicial set for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
            with the discrete or indiscrete filter, respectively.
          %p
          %h3#section_Metric_spaces:_uniformly_continuous_maps_and_quasi-isometries Metric spaces: uniformly continuous maps and quasi-isometries
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            be a metric space. View
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            as a simplicial set represented by the set of points of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^n
            with the filter generated by uniform neighbourhoods of the diagonal, i.e. subsets containing all tuples of small enough diameter. With this situs structure, a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M\to N
            is uniformly continuous iff it induces a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\bullet:M_\bullet\to N_\bullet
            of situses. In fact this defines a fully faithful embedding of the category of metric spaces with uniformly continuous maps into the category of situses.
          %p
            We can also consider a different situs structure capturing the notion of quasi-isomorphism in large scale geometry. Equip each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^n
            with the filter such that a subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^n
            is large iff it contains all
            = succeed "-tuples" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\geq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo •
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\bullet:M_\bullet\to N_\bullet
            is an isomorphism of situses iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M\to N
            is an quasi-isometry.
          %p
          %h3#section_A_filter_as_a_situs A filter as a situs
          %p
            Given a filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            on a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            viewed as a simplicial set (i.e. the simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X|_\bullet
            represented by
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X|_\bullet
            such that its filter on the set of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X^\mathfrak{F}|_\bullet^{\operatorname{cart}}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi 𝔉
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X^\mathfrak{F}|_\bullet^{\operatorname{diag}}
          %p In fact this gives two fully faithful embeddings of the category of filters into the category of situses
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mo −
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mo −
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %mo :
                  %mi ዋ
                  %mo →
                  %mi s
                  %mi ዋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-^\mathfrak{F}|_\bullet^{\operatorname{cart}}, |-^\mathfrak{F}|_\bullet^{\operatorname{diag}} : &amp;#x12CB;  \to s&amp;#x12CB;
          %p#PreordersAsSituses
            In a similar way one can define two fully faithful embeddings of the category of filters on preorders and continuous monotone maps. We denote these by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi 𝔉
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\leq\mathfrak{F}}_\bullet^{\operatorname{cart}}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi X
                      %mo •
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi 𝔉
                    %msup
                      %mo
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\leq\mathfrak{F}}_\bullet^{\operatorname{diag}}
          %p
          %h3#section_Topological_and_uniform_spaces_as_situses Topological and uniform spaces as situses
          %p#DiagCartDefnNdim
            More generally, given an arbitrary simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet
            and a filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            on the set of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet
            such that its filter on the set of
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
            and the filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            always indiscrete gives two fully faithful embeddings of the category of simplicial sets into the category of situses.
          %p We use this to define situses corresponding to uniform and topological spaces.
          %p
          %h4#section_Uniform_spaces_as_situses Uniform spaces as situses
          %p
            Take a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and view it as a simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet
            (represented by
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+space"} uniform structure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a filter on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            of
            = succeed "-simplices." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1,x_2\in X
            of points are “sufficiently close to each other” in the uniform structure iff the 1-simplex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_1,x_2)\in X\times X
            is “sufficiently small”.
          %p#SymmetricSitus
            In fact, it is easy to define uniform spaces in terms of situses. A filter on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            is a uniform structure iff it is symmetric (i.e. the endomorphism of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)\mapsto (y,x)
            permuting the coordinates is continuous) and this construction produces a situs such that the filter on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X\times X
            is the coarsest filter such that the two maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 3
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^3\to X\times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_1,x_2,x_3)\mapsto (x_i,x_{i+1}),i=1,2
            are continuous. This can be used to characterise situses arising from uniform structures as those symmetric situses such that the filter of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X\times X
            has this property. We say that a situs is symmetric iff it factors though the category of non-empty finite sets.
          %p
          %h4#section_Topological_spaces_as_situses Topological spaces as situses
          %p
            The situs associated to a topological structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is defined in the same way starting from the filter of non-uniform neighbourhoods of the diagonal on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times X
            defined as consisting of the subsets of form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup_{x\in X} \{x\}\times U_x
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi x
                  %mo ∋
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_x\ni x
            is a not necessarily open neighbourhood of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
          %p A trivial verification shows these constructions define fully faithful embeddings of the categories of topological and of uniform spaces into the category of situses, and in fact there are corresponding forgetful functors to these categories such that the following compositions are the identity:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi s
                  %mi ዋ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to s&amp;#x12CB; \to Top
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi UniformSpaces
                  %mo →
                  %mi s
                  %mi ዋ
                  %mo →
                  %mi UniformSpaces
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} UniformSpaces \to s&amp;#x12CB; \to UniformSpaces .
          %p
          %h3#section_Cauchy_sequences_and_equicontinuity Cauchy sequences and equicontinuity
          %p
            A filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            on a metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is
            %em Cauchy
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}^{\operatorname{cart}}_\bullet \to M_\bullet
            is continuous.
          %p
            A sequence of functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i:L \to M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in \mathbb{N}
            of metric spaces is
            %em uniformly equicontinuous
            iff the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi cofinite
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{ N}^{cofinite})^{\operatorname{diag}}_\bullet\times L_\bullet \to M_\bullet
            is continuous. This gives a precise meaning to the phrase “if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in\mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in L
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j\in \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in L
            are sufficiently close to each other, so are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i(x)
            and
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_j(y)
          %p
            A sequence of functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i:X \to M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in \mathbb {N}
            from a topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to a metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is
            %em equicontinuous
            iff the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi cofinite
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{ N}^{cofinite})^{\operatorname{diag}}_\bullet\times X_\bullet \to M_\bullet
            is continuous.
          %p
          %h3{:id => "section_Limits,_compactness,_and_completeness"} Limits, compactness, and completeness
          %p
            An endomorphism of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            of finite linear orders gives rise to an endomorphism of the category of situses. Of particular interest is the shift endomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta\to\Delta
            adding a new least element (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decalage"} decalage
            considers the endomorphism adding a new greatest element rather than least) on objects,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ↦
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\mapsto 1+n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:n\to m
            goes to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f[+1]:1+n\to 1+m
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f[+1](0)=0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f[+1](1+i)=1+f(i)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\leq i\leq n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet\circ [+1]
            and morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet\circ [+1]\to X
            allows one to talk about local properties of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet
          %p
          %h4#section_Limits_via_shift_endomorphism Limits via shift endomorphism
          %p
            For example, taking a
            %em limit
            of a filter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            on a topological or metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            corresponds to taking the factorization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}^{\operatorname{diag}}_\bullet\to X_\bullet\circ [+1]\to X_\bullet.
          %p
            Indeed, the underlying simplicial set of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} diag
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}^{\operatorname{diag}}_\bullet
            is connected and thus maps to a single connected component of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\bullet\circ [+1]=\sqcup_{x\in X} \{x\}\times X_\bullet
            (here we consider the equality of the underlying simplicial sets); continuity of the map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔉
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F} \to  X\times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\mapsto (x_0,x)
            means exactly that the first coordinate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            is a limit point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔉
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{F}
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
          %h4#section_Compactness_and_completeness_as_lifting_properties Compactness and completeness as lifting properties
          Diagram chasing reformulation of the notions above allows to define compactness and completeness as
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lifting_property"} lifting properties
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi cofinite
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{cofinite}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi cofinite
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\leq cofinite}
            denote the set, resp. the linear order
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^\leq
          %p
            A metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is
            %em complete
            iff either of the following equivalent conditions holds:
          %p
            i.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi cofinite
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                    %mo ⋌
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot\to (\mathbb{N}^{\leq cofinite})^{\operatorname{cart}}_\bullet \rightthreetimes M_\bullet\circ [+1]\to M_\bullet
          %p
            ii.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi cofinite
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                    %mo ⋌
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mo •
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot\to (\mathbb{N}^{cofinite})^{\operatorname{cart}}_\bullet \rightthreetimes M_\bullet\circ [+1]\to M_\bullet
          %p
            iii.
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi cofinite
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi cofinite
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                    %mo ⋌
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbb{N}^{\leq cofinite})^{\operatorname{cart}}_\bullet\to
                    (\mathbb{N}^{\leq cofinite}\cup\{\infty\})^{\operatorname{cart}}_\bullet  \rightthreetimes M_\bullet\to\top
          %p
            Such a reformulation raises the question whether the notion of completeness may be defined with help of the archetypal counterexample: is a metric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            complete iff
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo •
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo •
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋌
                      %mi lr
                  %mo ?
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_\bullet\circ [+1]\to M_\bullet \in (\mathbb {R}_\bullet\circ [+1]\to \mathbb{R}_\bullet)^{\rightthreetimes lr} ?
          %p It also allows to define the completion of a metric space in terms of a weak factorisation system
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi cofinite
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo •
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cart
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi cofinite
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
